WEBVTT


00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.981
There's a moment of orderly silence
before a football play begins.


00:00:32.981 --> 00:00:35.881
Players are in position,
linemen are frozen,


00:00:35.881 --> 00:00:37.381
and anything's possible.


00:00:37.381 --> 00:00:41.681
TV commentary


00:00:42.082 --> 00:00:46.282
Then, like a traffic accident,
staff begins to randomly collide.


00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:49.882
From the snap of the ball
to the snap of the first bone


00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:52.683
is closer to four seconds than five.


00:00:52.983 --> 00:00:58.183
TV commentary


00:01:00.983 --> 00:01:02.283
One Mississippi.


00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:04.484
Joe Theismann,
the Redskins quarterback


00:01:04.584 --> 00:01:06.784
takes the snap and hands off
to his running back.


00:01:06.884 --> 00:01:08.584
TV commentary


00:01:08.684 --> 00:01:10.284
Two Mississippi.


00:01:10.384 --> 00:01:12.684
It's a trick play, a flea flicker,


00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:15.785
and the running back tosses the ball
back to the quarterback.


00:01:15.785 --> 00:01:17.685
TV commentary


00:01:18.685 --> 00:01:19.885
Three Mississippi.


00:01:19.885 --> 00:01:24.085
Up to now the play's been defined
by the what the quarterback sees.


00:01:24.185 --> 00:01:27.286
It's about to be defined
by what he doesn't.


00:01:28.386 --> 00:01:29.586
Four Mississippi.


00:01:29.686 --> 00:01:33.386
Lawrence Taylor is the best
defensive player in the NFL


00:01:33.486 --> 00:01:37.087
and has been from the time he stepped
onto the field as a rookie.


00:01:37.087 --> 00:01:40.087
TV commentary


00:01:40.287 --> 00:01:44.887
TV commentary


00:01:45.087 --> 00:01:48.588
He will also change the game of football
as we know it.


00:01:48.588 --> 00:01:51.288
...And we'll look at it
with the reverse angle one more time.


00:01:51.388 --> 00:01:54.488
And I suggest if your stomach is weak
you just don't watch ...


00:01:54.488 --> 00:01:59.289
Legendary quarterback Joe Thiessman
never played another down of football.


00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:03.789
Now, y'all would guess
that more often than not


00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:07.289
the highest paid player on
an NFL team is the quarterback.


00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:08.690
And you'd be right.


00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.590
What you probably don't know
is that more often than not


00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.290
the second highest paid player is,
thanks to Lawrence Taylor,


00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.590
a left tackle.


00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.591
Because, as every housewife knows,


00:02:18.691 --> 00:02:20.791
the first check you write
is for the mortgage


00:02:20.791 --> 00:02:23.491
but the second is for the insurance.


00:02:23.591 --> 00:02:26.391
And the left tackle's job
is to protect the quarterback


00:02:26.391 --> 00:02:29.792
form what he can't see coming.
To protect his blind side.


00:02:30.192 --> 00:02:34.092
The ideal left tackle is big,
but a lot of people are big.


00:02:34.192 --> 00:02:37.092
He is wide in the butt
and massive in the thighs.


00:02:37.192 --> 00:02:41.393
He has long arms, giant hands
and feet as quick as a hiccup.


00:02:43.493 --> 00:02:46.193
This is a rare and expensive combination


00:02:46.193 --> 00:02:49.093
the need for which can be traced
to that Monday night game


00:02:49.093 --> 00:02:51.094
and Lawrence Taylor.


00:02:51.294 --> 00:02:55.094
For on that day he not only
altered Joe Theismann's life,


00:02:55.994 --> 00:02:57.594
but mine as well.


00:03:03.895 --> 00:03:05.395
Mr. Oher?


00:03:05.895 --> 00:03:07.395
Mr. Oher?


00:03:08.095 --> 00:03:11.295
Do you understand..?
Do you know why I'm here?


00:03:12.095 --> 00:03:13.896
